1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,960
Okay, moving on to the parathyroid gland.

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:04,400
Recall that the key cell within the parathyroid gland

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:05,400
is called the chief cell.

4
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:09,420
Its goal is to regulate the serum-free ionized calcium.

5
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:12,480
Remember that we're talking about free calcium,

6
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,480
and I emphasize that because 60% of the calcium

7
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,240
in your blood is bound to proteins,

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,880
so we're not worried about that.

9
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,560
And among the unbound calcium,

10
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:22,940
some of it is bound to phosphate.

11
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:24,440
We're also not worried about that.

12
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:27,920
This is going to regulate the free ionized calcium,

13
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,540
and the way by which it does it is by a PTH.

14
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,000
Now, when the free ionized calcium is low within the blood,

15
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:35,840
then PTH will be released,

16
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,160
and PTH acts on three major tissues in the body

17
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:40,640
to raise the calcium.

18
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,920
First, it will increase the activity of bone osteoclasts.

19
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,020
Now, recall that osteoclasts are the cells that resorb bone,

20
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:48,400
and so when they resorb bone,

21
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:51,680
both calcium and phosphate will be released into the blood.

22
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:53,520
Now, one high-yield point here

23
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:57,400
is that the PTH doesn't directly activate the osteoclast.

24
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,700
It actually activates the osteoblast,

25
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:02,020
which is the cell that lays down bone,

26
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:04,120
and once it activates the osteoblast,

27
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,760
the osteoblast then goes and activates the osteoclast,

28
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:10,040
and that's particularly high-yield to note.

29
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:12,700
So again, PTH activates the osteoblast,

30
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,100
which then in turn activates the osteoclast.

31
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:17,260
The osteoclast then resorbs bone,

32
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:19,780
releasing calcium and phosphate into the blood.

33
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,040
The second function of PTH

34
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,240
will be to increase small bowel absorption

35
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,420
of calcium and phosphate.

36
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:26,860
This is a little bit indirect.

37
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:29,060
Actually, PTH activates vitamin D,

38
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,800
then vitamin D then increases small bowel absorption

39
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:32,900
of calcium and phosphate.

40
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:34,400
And the third function of PTH

41
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,700
will be to grab calcium from the kidney

42
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:38,080
and to dump phosphate

43
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:40,520
so that you actually have decreased phosphate resorption.

44
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:42,240
So you'll grab calcium and dump phosphate.

45
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:44,720
This is a particularly noteworthy step

46
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:46,440
because by dumping the phosphate,

47
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:48,260
that's how you're going to really increase

48
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:49,480
the free ionized calcium.

49
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:52,020
Otherwise, this was bringing in both calcium and phosphate,

50
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:53,820
and this brought in both calcium and phosphate,

51
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:57,040
but you really want to increase the free ionized calcium.

52
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,420
And so this, again, is a very important function.

53
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:02,000
Now, the first disorder of the parathyroid

54
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,900
is primary hyperparathyroidism.

55
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:06,920
This is where you have excess parathyroid hormone

56
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,600
due to a disorder of the parathyroid gland itself.

57
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:12,680
Now, you can actually get primary hyperparathyroidism

58
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:13,400
in three ways.

59
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:14,860
You can get an adenoma,

60
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,660
which would be a tumor that's going to produce

61
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:17,960
too much parathyroid hormone.

62
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,540
You could have a sporadic hyperplasia

63
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:23,020
where the cells just hyperplase for an unknown reason.

64
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:26,020
That could also result in too much parathyroid hormone.

65
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:28,560
And the third possibility would be carcinoma.

66
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,300
You could get a parathyroid carcinoma,

67
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:31,900
which would then produce too much PTH.

68
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:36,100
However, the most common cause is parathyroid adenoma.

69
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:39,360
Parathyroid adenoma, again, is a benign neoplasm,

70
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:40,720
usually involving one gland.

71
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,220
It will produce PTH,

72
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,040
and that PTH will then, of course,

73
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:45,080
raise the serum calcium.

74
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:47,020
However, it's usually asymptomatic.

75
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:48,760
If, however, symptoms are present,

76
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,900
they will be based on the consequences of increased PTH

77
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:54,400
and on the consequences of hypercalcemia.

78
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:57,640
For example, if patients have hypercalcemia in the blood,

79
00:02:57,720 --> 00:02:59,620
there's going to be a high filtration of calcium

80
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:00,360
into the urine,

81
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:02,780
and so patients can develop kidney stones

82
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:04,000
or nephrolithiasis,

83
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:05,600
and the classic kidney stone

84
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,320
would be a calcium oxalate stone.

85
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:08,960
So this would be one example

86
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,700
of a consequence of hypercalcemia.

87
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:12,720
Another consequence of hypercalcemia

88
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,960
would be that when you have high levels of calcium

89
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:16,960
within the blood, you increase the risk

90
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:18,960
for deposition of calcium on tissues,

91
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:20,820
which is called metastatic calcification.

92
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:23,420
The classic example of metastatic calcification

93
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:24,960
is called nephrocalcinosis,

94
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:28,300
where you deposit calcium in the tubules of the kidney.

95
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:31,340
This can lead to renal insufficiency with polyuria,

96
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:33,920
and so it's another consequence of hypercalcemia.

97
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:37,100
In addition, the hypercalcemia can go into the CNS

98
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:38,560
and cause CNS disturbances,

99
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:40,080
such as depression and seizures.

100
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:42,840
Hypercalcemia can also lead to constipation,

101
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:45,040
peptic ulcer disease, and acute pancreatitis.

102
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:48,140
This is particularly high-yield hypercalcemia

103
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:49,680
producing an acute pancreatitis.

104
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,920
The way I remember it is that if you have high calcium,

105
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:54,300
high calcium is sort of an enzyme activator,

106
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:56,500
and so you can activate the enzymes of the pancreas

107
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:57,440
within the pancreas,

108
00:03:57,780 --> 00:03:59,280
giving you an acute pancreatitis.

109
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,780
And finally, because you've got high levels of PTH,

110
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:03,580
you're gonna have massive resorption of bone.

111
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:06,000
You can eventually produce fibrosis and cysts

112
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:06,560
within the bone,

113
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,600
which is called osteitis fibrosa cystica.

114
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:11,460
Now here is a picture of the kidney

115
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,060
showing the tubules of the kidney,

116
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,620
and you can clearly see that there's deposition of calcium

117
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:16,900
within some of these tubules,

118
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:19,800
and so this is an example of metastatic calcification

119
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,460
in a patient with hypercalcemia,

120
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:24,460
and of course, this would then be called nephrocalcinosis.

121
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:26,200
An additional consequence would be

122
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,620
to get fibrosis of the bone,

123
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,860
and so here you can see this is clearly a spicule of bone,

124
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:33,700
and you've got this dense fibrosis,

125
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,040
and sometimes you can see cysts and hemorrhage as well,

126
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:38,540
and that's called osteitis fibrosa cystica,

127
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,840
again, a consequence of the high PTH.

128
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:43,900
The lab findings in primary hyperparathyroidism

129
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:45,320
will be a high serum PTH.

130
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:48,460
Of course, the adenoma, for example, is making PTH.

131
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:50,400
You'll have a high serum calcium,

132
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:52,040
but a decreased serum phosphate.

133
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:54,900
Of course, the high serum calcium is due to the fact

134
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:55,940
that you're resorbing bone,

135
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:58,140
increasing absorption from the gut,

136
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,660
and also increasing resorption from the kidney,

137
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,120
and the low serum phosphate is due to the fact

138
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:04,880
that you have decreased resorption in the kidney,

139
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:08,420
or you're quote-unquote dumping phosphate into the urine.

140
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:10,900
You can also have an increased urinary cyclic AMP.

141
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:12,820
This is very high yield for the board exam,

142
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:14,840
so let me just review this physiology briefly.

143
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:16,860
Remember that if this is the cell

144
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:18,380
that's responding to the PTH,

145
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:20,700
then you've sort of got this receptor here,

146
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:22,860
and PTH will bind this receptor.

147
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,400
Let's, for example, take the kidney.

148
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:27,840
Now, when PTH binds the tubular cells of the kidney,

149
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:29,700
then that is going to be coupled

150
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:31,960
to a G-stimulatory protein.

151
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:34,700
That G-stimulatory protein will activate

152
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:36,980
adenylate cyclase, very high yield,

153
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:40,660
and that adenylate cyclase will then convert ATP

154
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:43,780
into cyclic AMP,

155
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:48,680
and so cyclic AMP will then mediate the effects of the PTH,

156
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:52,780
and so cyclic AMP can be increased in the urine

157
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:54,520
because you've got this PTH,

158
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:55,740
which is hitting the tubules,

159
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:57,200
and so you've got increased cyclic AMP

160
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:59,540
within the tubular cells,

161
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:00,860
which can then get into the urine.

162
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:03,580
Very high yield for the purposes of board exams

163
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:04,860
because it takes you back

164
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:06,320
to a little bit of normal physiology,

165
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:07,620
which they love to tie in.

166
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,860
Finally, the patients can have an increase

167
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:11,860
in the serum alkaline phosphatase,

168
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,880
and this is a particularly high yield point as well.

169
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,600
Now, alkaline phosphatase is an enzyme

170
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:19,600
that actually generates an alkaline environment

171
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:20,920
in the region of the bone,

172
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:23,260
and the alkaline environment is necessary

173
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:25,420
to lay down calcium on bone.

174
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:27,660
And so you could ask the question,

175
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:29,600
why is there an increase in the alkaline phosphatase

176
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:31,100
when you're actually resorbing bone

177
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:32,380
and actually removing calcium?

178
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:33,980
Well, the answer is that remember

179
00:06:33,980 --> 00:06:37,100
that the PTH doesn't directly activate the osteoclast.

180
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:38,660
It actually activates the osteoblast,

181
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:40,660
which is going to lay down bone,

182
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:42,940
and at the same time turn on the osteoclast,

183
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:43,860
which will eat up bone.

184
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:47,780
And so the osteoblast is actually going to be turned on.

185
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:49,340
It's going to lay down bone,

186
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:50,760
and when it lays down bone,

187
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:52,580
it actually produces alkaline phosphatase.

188
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:54,500
So the alkaline phosphatase is actually a sign

189
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:56,280
of osteoblastic activity,

190
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:57,640
and that's because, of course,

191
00:06:57,720 --> 00:06:59,940
the PTH receptors are on the osteoblast,

192
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:01,460
so that becomes high yield as well.

193
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:04,220
The treatment is surgical removal of the affected gland,

194
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:06,040
and so, for example, if there's an adenoma,

195
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:07,920
you'll simply remove that adenoma.

196
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:10,160
Secondary hyperparathyroidism

197
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:12,060
is when you have excess production of PTH.

198
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:14,180
However, it's due to a disease process

199
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:16,680
that is extrinsic to the parathyroid gland,

200
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:19,100
i.e. there is some other disease process

201
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:21,840
that results in the overproduction of PTH.

202
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:24,540
The most common cause is chronic renal failure.

203
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:26,980
The idea here is that when you have renal failure,

204
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:29,080
you're going to have decreased excretion of phosphate.

205
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:31,140
Phosphate will then build up in the blood.

206
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:33,620
Phosphate loves to bind free calcium,

207
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:35,700
and so phosphate will bind free calcium,

208
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,080
therefore the free calcium levels will decrease.

209
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:40,080
Now remember that the parathyroid gland

210
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,000
doesn't look at anything except free calcium,

211
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:44,460
and so when it sees that there's less free calcium,

212
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:47,340
that's going to stimulate all four parathyroid glands

213
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,200
to increase PTH secretion,

214
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:52,640
and that then will result in a secondary hyperparathyroidism.

215
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,540
The lab findings will be an increase in PTH,

216
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:58,400
and they'll have a decrease in their serum calcium.

217
00:07:58,820 --> 00:07:59,220
Why?

218
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:01,180
Because remember that this process is driven

219
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:02,800
by the fact that there's high phosphate,

220
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:03,860
and when there's high phosphate,

221
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,600
the serum calcium will actually be low,

222
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:08,420
and so there'll be a low serum calcium

223
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:09,920
and an increased serum phosphate.

224
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:11,040
At the same time,

225
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:12,680
there'll be a high alkaline phosphatase,

226
00:08:12,740 --> 00:08:14,560
and why is there a high alkaline phosphatase?

227
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:16,760
Because the PTH is hitting the bone,

228
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:18,880
in particular the osteoblasts,

229
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:20,600
and so the osteoblasts are overactive,

230
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:23,200
and so you've got a high alkaline phosphatase.

231
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:25,820
Moving on to hypoparathyroidism,

232
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:27,320
this is when you have a low PTH.

233
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,840
The causes include autoimmune damage,

234
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:31,200
you can have autoimmune destruction

235
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:32,440
of the parathyroid glands,

236
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:33,760
surgical excision,

237
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:36,140
for example if the patient has removal of a thyroid

238
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:38,180
and the parathyroid gets taken by accident,

239
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:41,420
or for example if there is parathyroid hyperplasia

240
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:42,860
and the parathyroids are removed.

241
00:08:43,220 --> 00:08:44,460
And finally, DeGeorge syndrome.

242
00:08:44,460 --> 00:08:46,880
Remember in DeGeorge syndrome you get failure to develop

243
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:48,380
the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch,

244
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:51,180
and of course the parathyroid are also a derivative

245
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:53,260
of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouch.

246
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:55,520
The presentation would be based on the fact

247
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:57,340
that there would be a low serum-free calcium,

248
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:59,660
and so you'd get numbness and tingling,

249
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:01,200
that's a very early sign,

250
00:09:01,320 --> 00:09:03,540
particularly perioral around the lips,

251
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:05,880
and then they can also get muscle spasms,

252
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:08,980
and the classic examples of this would be the Trousseau sign,

253
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:10,580
where you feel the blood pressure cuff

254
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:12,480
on the patient's arm and you get muscle spasms,

255
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:14,960
or you could get Chosevic's sign where you tap

256
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:17,300
on the facial nerve and also get muscle spasms.

257
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:20,760
Pseudohypoparathyroidism is when you have

258
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:23,300
the signs and symptoms of hypoparathyroidism,

259
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:27,100
however it is not due to a problem with the parathyroid,

260
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,620
instead it is due to a problem with the organs

261
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:31,340
not responding to PTH.

262
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:34,640
And so in this particular case you'll have hypocalcemia,

263
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:36,800
however the PTH levels will be high,

264
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:39,180
and that's because the parathyroid is making PTH.

265
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:41,540
Now particularly high yield to note,

266
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:43,860
and sometimes asked on board examinations,

267
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,340
and that is that the end organ resistance here

268
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:51,780
is actually due to a defect in the G-stimulatory protein.

269
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:54,860
Again, this goes back to our normal diagram,

270
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:57,520
if this was the cell and this was the receptor for PTH,

271
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:01,240
again when PTH binds it's associated with a G-protein,

272
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:04,760
and particularly there's a G-stimulatory protein here.

273
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:08,960
This G-stimulatory protein will then turn on adenylate cyclase,

274
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:10,760
and of course when you turn on adenylate cyclase,

275
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:14,100
that helps to take ATP to generate cyclic AMP,

276
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:15,740
which is a key messenger.

277
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:19,500
Now what happens in one subtype of pseudo-hypoparathyroidism

278
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:22,520
is that you have a defective GS protein here,

279
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:24,920
and that's particularly high yield board examiners

280
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:26,940
love to go after it because it's really a way

281
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:28,860
by which they can test you on normal physiology.

282
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:32,280
Now in that particular subtype of pseudo-hypoparathyroidism,

283
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:34,000
it's an autosomal dominant form,

284
00:10:34,120 --> 00:10:36,080
and it's also associated with short stature

285
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:37,900
and short fourth and fifth digits.

286
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:39,960
So that's a high yield association as well,

287
00:10:40,180 --> 00:10:41,880
particularly for board examinations.

288
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:44,300
And that concludes our section on parathyroid.

